  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "NqS9+QyDVrZ9rOE/Px0R4/4kPAM=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:33:40.179Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "## The flow\n\n**1. On login** the client mints a bearer token (`renown.getBearerToken()`) and stores it \u2014 with a `RenownSessionProfile` display hint (`name`/`avatar` plus `documentId`/`username`/`userImage`) \u2014 in an **HttpOnly cookie**. The name is exported as `RENOWN_SESSION_COOKIE` and the value is built with `serializeRenownSessionCookie`. `RenownProvider`'s session-cookie sync does this automatically when given a `session`. Type the route handler with the exported `RenownSessionCookie` rather than redeclaring the payload shape.\n\n**2. A Data Access Layer** reads the cookie and calls `verifyRenownSession`, memoized per request:\n\n```typescript\nimport { RENOWN_SESSION_COOKIE, verifyRenownSession } from \"@renown/sdk/node\";\n\nconst cookie = (await cookies()).get(RENOWN_SESSION_COOKIE)?.value;\nreturn (await verifyRenownSession(cookie, { switchboardUrl: SWITCHBOARD_URL })) ?? null;\n```\n\n## What verification actually checks\n\nBy default `verifyRenownSession` verifies the **JWT only** \u2014 signature and expiry \u2014 and merges the display hint into `user` as *unverified, display-only* data, rebuilding `user.ens` and, when `documentId` is present, `user.profile`.\n\nPass **`verifyCredential: true`** to also re-check the credential against the Switchboard, so a **revoked or expired credential is rejected**. It defaults to `false`, which is token-only and does no network call. That default is the gotcha: a revoked credential still presents a valid, unexpired JWT, so the default path keeps admitting a user whose credential has been pulled until the token itself expires.\n\n## The one thing never to use for access control\n\n`readSessionClaims(cookieValue)` gives a cheap, **signature-free** decode. It is for optimistic checks only \u2014 an edge redirect, for instance. Never trust it for access control: an attacker can author any claims they like in a cookie they control, and a signature-free decode will happily return them.\n\n_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/04-Reference/06-Authorization/renown-sdk/01-Authentication.md (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-13T14:33:39.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-13T14:33:40.179Z"
    }
  },
